ily  that's about as far as finding a traffic  light in midtown manhattan  and what are the penalties if found  guilty of that sort of the charge  well the racketeering charge could put  donald in prison for the rest of his  life if a judge were to sentence him to  prison  uh he's likely to be charged under the  martin act with  business records uh misuse  that's a felony that can get you i think  it's 25 years  donald is looking at the prospect upon  conviction  of what amounts to a life sentence  whether or not he would actually go to  prison would be up to a judge  who could well decide that home  confinement would be  sufficient hopefully one would think  home confinement in trump tower not  mar-a-lago given that he's going to be  tried in new york  and what would it mean in terms of the  financial situation for the trump  organization  well one of the things we don't know is  what is the financial situation of the  trump organization  it's a house of cards and donald has  always been  desperate for cash money just runs  through his fingers the way  water does through a hose and  so legal fees alone are going to  just consume uh money from donald trump  on the other hand he's raised several  hundred million dollars by essentially  begging  to the public and well he said the money  was to  overturn the election he's only spent  about nine million dollars on lawyers  for that purpose  so he's got a nest egg he can use for  this of a couple hundred million dollars  and i'm sure there are supporters who  will send him  more money but any first-rate criminal  defense lawyer who's going to take on  this case  knowing donald's history of not paying  his lawyers is probably going to demand  a  very very big fee  from your point of view and we know  donald trump has denied any wrongdoing  and he's called this a political witch  hunt by both the  manhattan district attorney and the new  york state attorney general  from your point of view what would be  the most impressive or effective defense  that he could raise oh on my  my staff i didn't realize what they did  or i mean i just told them  do what the law requires and i can't  believe they went and did these  outrageous things and they never told me  about it  that's not a credible defense but that's  about as solid  offenses he's going to be able to come  up with donald like  several very prominent people on wall  street does not  use email and he speaks not only in code  but through other people  there's a particular uh leader of one of  the hundred biggest companies in america  who talks to an assistant who talks to  assistants  and that's why no prosecutor's ever been  able to get him even though  it's widely understood and there's some  public record indicating he's a major  league criminal  and that's going to be donald's defense  is i i tell these people to push the law  as far as you can  but no further then  i take it that's the importance then of  the district attorney going after his  chief  financial officer mr weissel that's  right and if they file a racketeering  charge that would be against donald  trump  against the trump organization and  people affiliated with it that would  include his three adult children who  were employees of it  and especially allen weiselberg who was  the  quote-unquote chief financial officer  it's not that big an operation  he was the money guy all the way back to  fred trump  can you actually imagine a former  president of the united states being  sent  to prison in a new york state prison for  these crimes  um it would be uh surprising um  i think that a judge would think heavily  about the implications of the country on  the other hand  there are the countries all around the  world that have prosecuted and  imprisoned  former presidents i'm not talking about  dictatorships uh we've seen this in  france uh italy  uh we may momentarily see it in israel  and we've seen it i think once in the  past in israel  there is no reason we should t